  "faq.q.flagged": "What happens if my provider is flagged?",
  "faq.q.conductNotice": "How would I know if a case was opened about my provider?",
  "faq.a.conductNotice": "Go to your provider page and connect the wallet that claimed the listing. There is a \"Check for notices\" panel: sign, and it shows any open conduct case against you in full, including the grounds and the evidence. The panel is there whether or not a case exists, so nobody watching can infer anything from seeing it. If you have set a notice email on your listing we also email that address when a case opens, but the panel is the channel that works even if you have not.",
  "faq.q.watchFlags": "Can I be notified if a new provider is flagged?",
  "faq.q.logo": "How do logos work, and what if one is inappropriate?",
  "faq.q.addresses": "Which of my addresses should I use?",
  "faq.q.api": "Is there an API?",
  "faq.q.cost": "Does it cost anything?",
  "faq.q.who": "Who runs Flare Registry?",
  "faq.a.list": "Connect the wallet for your registered FTSO address and sign a message to prove you control it, then fill in your details on the [submit] page. Your provider page is available right away. New providers are held from the listed feed for 30 days after claiming, so the Management Group has time to review before you reach wallets; after that you list automatically once you meet the criteria, unless the Management Group has opened a case (providers already listed before this policy are unaffected).",
  "faq.q.claim": "How do I claim a listing that's already here?",
  "faq.a.claim": "Some entries are seeded from the legacy provider list and are not yet claimed by their owner. To take ownership, connect the wallet for one of that listing's registered addresses and sign in on the [submit] page. The form loads the existing entry so you can update its details and logo; saving claims it under your wallet and it is no longer marked as an unclaimed seed. Claiming an entry that was already on the registry does not restart its listing; the 30-day new-provider hold applies only to genuinely new listings.",
  "faq.a.qualified": "Qualified is a status computed automatically from Flare's on-chain reward data. It means you meet every criterion: registered, actively submitting, sufficient vote power, and uptime. Each criterion is shown as a pass/fail on your provider page. New providers that already meet every criterion are still held from Qualified and the listed feed for 30 days after claiming, so the Management Group can review; the badge then appears automatically, unless the Management Group has opened a case, which holds it until the case concludes.",
  "faq.a.notQualified": "Open your provider page and check the qualification checklist: any criterion marked with a red mark explains exactly what is missing (for example uptime below the threshold, or not submitting in the latest epoch). If every criterion passes but the badge is still absent, you are inside the 30-day new-provider hold: the page shows the date you list automatically. If the Management Group has opened a case, the hold continues until the case concludes, and the page shows the case instead of a date. Qualified is earned by meeting all criteria and, for new providers, clearing that window.",
  "faq.a.loseQualified": "Qualified is sticky. Once earned it stays, even if a single metric dips. The only way to lose it is to stop submitting prices for 60 days, after which you re-qualify from scratch. Your provider page shows how many epochs remain before that. (Earning it the first time: a new provider that meets every criterion is held for 30 days after claiming before the badge appears.)",
  "faq.a.registered": "Yes. On Flare and Songbird you must already be a registered FTSO signal provider on-chain before you can list; Flare Registry reads your existing registration and does not register you with Flare.",
  "faq.a.mgBadge": "It marks members of Flare's on-chain FTSO Management Group, a curated set of eligible, well-performing providers that Flare itself maintains. Flare Registry reads it live from the contract.",
  "faq.a.flagged": "A new provider can be flagged by Management Group members for evidence-based reasons (plagiarism, impersonation, fraud) through a transparent, public vote. One member's flag is just an unconfirmed marker; it takes two members to open a case. See the [governance] page for the full process.",
  "faq.a.watchFlags": "Yes. On any new provider's page (while it is in its review window) there is a \"notify me\" box: enter your email, confirm via the link we send, and we will email you if that provider is flagged, if a case opens, and when it concludes. No wallet or account is needed. Your email is kept only while the provider is under review, then deleted, and every notice has a one-click unsubscribe. New providers are also offered this when they first list.",
  "faq.a.logo": "When you upload or change a logo, it is held for a 7-day review window before it goes live, so nothing appears instantly. Your current logo keeps showing during that time, and your listing ownership still activates right away; only the new image waits. Every upload is reviewed, and Management Group members can report an inappropriate logo from the provider page; reports are kept on record and an administrator can remove the logo.",
  "faq.a.addresses": "An FTSO entity registers several addresses (identity, submit, submit-signatures, signing-policy, delegation). Any of them is accepted, so sign in with whichever wallet is most convenient and that you are comfortable signing with.",
  "faq.a.api": "Yes. Flare Registry serves a documented, public JSON API and a feed compatible with the standard provider-list schema, so wallets and apps can build on it. See the [api] page.",
  "faq.q.siteStale": "I'm Qualified, but a website still shows outdated data or doesn't list me. What can I do?",
  "faq.a.siteStale": "First, if you are a new provider, confirm you are past the 30-day new-provider hold: until it ends you are not in the listed feed yet, so no site will show you regardless. Your provider page shows the date you list. Otherwise: Flare Registry publishes your status, but each third-party website chooses which list it loads. If a wallet or explorer shows stale information or doesn't list you, it's almost always because it uses an older or hand-maintained list, not ours. You can't edit their list yourself, but you can ask them to adopt Flare Registry: our feed is compatible with the standard schema, so for them it's a single URL change. Share the [api] page with their team.",
  "faq.q.askAdoption": "How do I ask a wallet or explorer to use Flare Registry?",
  "faq.a.askAdoption": "Reach out to their team and point out they can load our public feed instead of their current list. The argument is simple: our feed is a compatible superset of the standard provider-list schema, so any site that already reads that list can read Flare Registry with no code change, just by pointing at our URL. On top of that they get verified Qualified status, logos, and up-to-date on-chain data. The [api] page has the feed URL and an example you can forward.",
  "faq.a.cost": "Listing and qualification on Flare Registry are free. Note that on-chain FTSO operations themselves (which happen on Flare, not here) have their own costs.",
  "faq.a.who": "Flare Registry is built by Burst Labs and is open source, including the qualification rules that decide listing. Learn more on the [why] page.",
