  "card.telegram": "Providers' Telegram group",
  "card.reputation": "Reputation",
  "rep.intro": "A composite over Flare's own measurements. Every input is published by Flare, the weights below are fixed and versioned, and scoring is absolute rather than against other providers, so nobody's figure moves when a competitor's does.",
  "rep.tip.score": "The weighted total of the components below, on a 0 to 100 scale. Scored against the protocol's own scale, never against other providers, so this figure does not move when a competitor improves or declines. Clean record is 95 and above with no strike and no ineligible epoch in the window, Strong is 95 and above, Solid 85, Mixed 80, below that Needs attention.",
  "rep.tip.reliability": "Reward epochs in which Flare judged this provider eligible for rewards, out of the last 30 that carry a verdict. This is Flare's own per-epoch eligibleForReward from passes.json, counted, not our assessment.  Failing it means the protocol withheld that epoch's rewards entirely, so it is the heaviest component. Recent epochs count for more: the weighting halves roughly every 10 epochs, so a miss last week costs several times what the same miss costs at the far end of the window. A flat rate would say a provider who broke three months ago and one who is broken now are identical, when one has a fixed problem and the other a live one.",
  "rep.tip.longevity": "Reward epochs in which this entity has been seen registered. Counted rather than measured from the first epoch, so a spell of being deregistered does not quietly count as tenure. It reaches full marks at 100 epochs, about a year, and stops counting after that: staying is evidence, starting early is not.",
  "rep.tip.validators": "Uptime of the validator nodes this entity runs, as Flare's own P-chain reports it. Averaged across all of the entity's nodes, and the lowest reading seen in each epoch is the one kept, so a drop that recovers within the epoch still counts. Providers that run no validator are not scored on this at all rather than scored zero.",
  "rep.validators.ramping": "Weight still growing: {n} of {full} epochs of uptime history recorded so far.",
  "rep.validators.notYet": "Measured but not yet counted: {n} of {full} epochs of uptime history recorded. This component contributes nothing to the score, up or down, until there is enough history behind it to mean something.",
  "rep.band.strong": "Strong",
  "repdoc.bands.cleanWhy": "Clean record is not a higher score than Strong, and the two overlap on purpose. It is the same range plus a fact about the record: Flare recorded no failed minimal condition and no ineligible reward epoch in any epoch of the window. There is no room for another line on the score itself at that end of the scale, because the providers there sit at full marks on reward eligibility, longevity and implementation independence alike, and the differences that remain between them are the same size as the movement from one epoch to the next. A line drawn there would report a ranking the data does not contain. This states something a threshold cannot: a single fact a provider controls, that changes only when a real failure happens or a full window passes without one.",
  "repdoc.bands.cleanRule": "{n} and above, with no strike and no ineligible epoch in the window",
  "rep.band.clean": "Clean record",
  "rep.band.solid": "Solid",
  "rep.band.mixed": "Mixed",
  "rep.band.attention": "Needs attention",
  "rep.of100": "{score} out of 100",
  "rep.comp.reliability": "Reward eligibility",
  "rep.comp.longevity": "Longevity",
  "rep.comp.validators": "Validator uptime",
  "rep.comp.conditions": "Minimal conditions",
  "rep.comp.strikes": "Strikes",
  "rep.total": "Total",
  "rep.pts": "pts",
  "rep.cond.ftso": "FTSO scaling",
  "rep.cond.fdc": "FDC voting",
  "rep.cond.fast": "Fast updates",
  "rep.cond.staking": "Staking",
  "rep.cond.met": "met",
  "rep.cond.asOf": "as of epoch {epoch}",
  "rep.cond.partial": "met in {pct}% of epochs",
  "rep.tip.conditions": "The four jobs Flare asks of a provider, as rates rather than pass or fail: FTSO scaling hits, FDC voting rounds, fast updates, and staking. Averaged over the same 30 reward epochs, with recent epochs counting for more on the same half-life as reward eligibility, so the figure carries the direction of travel rather than only the average. Age is counted in epochs the provider actually published, so going quiet does not age a bad run away. This replaced a component built on Flare's reward-band success rates. The primary band is tight, so that component paid for closeness to the consensus median rather than for doing the work well. These figures come from a published file with years of history that anyone can replay, instead of a live gauge with no epoch and no way to check last month.",
  "rep.tip.strikes": "Flare's own strike count, from a file it publishes every reward epoch. A strike is one of the four minimal conditions failing in that epoch, so the number is that epoch's severity rather than a running tally: nothing accumulates and nothing is worked off. Four can fail at once. The heaviest strike in the last 30 epochs drives the component, discounted by age on the same 10-epoch half-life as reward eligibility, so a strike this epoch costs full price while one from the far end of the window costs about a seventh. Age is counted in epochs the provider actually turned up for, not calendar epochs, so going quiet stalls recovery rather than earning it. Both figures are shown: the count Flare recorded, and the discounted value the score uses.",
  "rep.comp.independence": "Implementation independence",
  "rep.raw.excluded": "independent implementation verified",
  "rep.raw.candidate": "matches a shared-implementation pattern",
  "rep.raw.other-median": "no match to the reference pattern",
  "rep.tip.independence": "Mirrored from an independent screen at oracleindependence.com that compares how providers' submitted values are distributed. That screen states its own limits and they apply here: it is a suspicion score rather than proof, it holds zero confirmed positives, and its signal is one-sided. A low reading is strong evidence that a provider runs its own implementation; a high reading proves nothing, because any median-of-prints implementation reads high whether or not it is the reference code. This component is therefore lopsided on purpose, giving credit for verified independence and deducting very little otherwise, and it carries the smallest weight of anything here.  A second, unaffiliated measurement from cerberusonchain.xyz is used alongside it: where both methods independently point the same way, the deduction is larger, because two screens sharing no signals is the one case worth more than either alone. Read it as a prompt to go and look, never as a finding.",
  "rep.comp.longevityRaw": "{epochs} reward epochs registered",
  "rep.weight": "{weight} pts",
